WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.365
Replication introduces two separate architectural
questions: where reads can run, and which machine

00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:08.359
is authorized to accept writes.

00:00:08.359 --> 00:00:12.473
We can scale the first without automatically solving
the second.

00:00:12.473 --> 00:00:15.945
Start by classifying the promises made to each
reader.

00:00:15.945 --> 00:00:21.089
A tutoring service updates a student’s appointment
time on the primary database.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.533
A replica that has not yet applied the change still
returns the old time.

00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:30.816
The write can have succeeded even though an
immediately refreshed page looks unchanged.

00:00:30.816 --> 00:00:36.889
Historical appointment reports may tolerate this
delay; the confirmation shown to the student should

00:00:36.889 --> 00:00:40.047
follow a path that satisfies the promised freshness.

00:00:40.047 --> 00:00:44.667
Imagine one primary handling 400 writes and 3,000
reads per second.

00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:51.217
Moving 2,400 eligible reads to three replicas leaves
roughly 600 reads on the primary, but each

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:54.113
replica must still apply the write stream.

00:00:54.113 --> 00:00:57.698
This is a workload sketch, not a capacity guarantee.

00:00:57.698 --> 00:01:04.386
If the primary fails, promoting a standby requires
establishing a single writer and understanding

00:01:04.386 --> 00:01:07.007
which acknowledged writes it contains.

00:01:07.007 --> 00:01:11.971
A timeout alone does not establish that the previous
writer has stopped.

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:16.485
Sharding distributes different records rather than
making full copies.

00:01:16.485 --> 00:01:20.031
Its benefit depends on keeping common operations
local.

00:01:20.031 --> 00:01:26.222
Let us follow a customer query, then compare it with
a global report using a deliberately simple

00:01:26.222 --> 00:01:27.189
capacity model.

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:31.058
A maintenance platform shards equipment records by
customer.

00:01:31.058 --> 00:01:37.055
Fetching one customer’s inspection history stays on
a single shard, while a report across all

00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:40.859
customers contacts several shards and merges their
results.

00:01:40.871 --> 00:01:47.469
Placing customers evenly by count can still overload
a shard containing a huge customer; traffic and

00:01:47.469 --> 00:01:50.570
record size matter as well as the routing rule.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:56.837
In a simplified balanced model, eight shards each
provide 500 equal-cost operations per second.

00:01:56.837 --> 00:02:02.049
Requests touching one shard have an upper bound near
4,000 requests per second.

00:02:02.071 --> 00:02:06.792
If each request touches four shards, the bound drops
to about 1,000.

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:09.639
If it touches all eight, it drops to 500.

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:16.235
Parallel requests may finish sooner than serial
requests, but each still consumes cluster work.

00:02:16.235 --> 00:02:22.275
Cross-shard updates additionally need an explicit
atomicity or reconciliation strategy.
